\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Transport of exact interacting laws}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{Let #{O:(H,h)\to(H',h')} be a linear isometric isomorphism,
with transported full data
##{Q'=OQO^{-1},\qquad P'=OPO^{-1},\qquad w'\circ O=w.}
For the \vocabk{weighted Gaussian laws}{fdip-00GH} and
\vocabk{exact fiber interactions}{fdip-00GH}, one has
##{\begin{aligned}
\mathcal Z_{Q'}[w']&=\mathcal Z_Q[w],&
O_*\nu_{Q,w}&=\nu_{Q',w'},\\
(\mathcal R_{Q'}^{OX}w')(Ox)&=(\mathcal R_Q^Xw)(x),\\
\mathcal F_{Q'}^{OX}[w'](Ox)&=\mathcal F_Q^X[w](x).
\end{aligned}}
The Euclidean constants #{c_Q,c_C,c_S} also agree with their
transported counterparts. For a polynomial interaction the weight
condition follows from #{V'\circ O=V} and the same real #{\lambda}.
If #{O} transports each subspace of a supplied orthogonal flag,
these identities hold at every stage of \ref{fdip-00GK}.}

\proof{
\p{The restrictions of #{O} to #{X,Y} preserve the induced
Euclidean volumes. Their block conjugacies carry #{S,C} to #{S',C'}
and carry the mean #{-C^{-1}B^\dagger x} to the corresponding mean
at #{Ox}. Thus they transport the explicit conditional Gaussian
kernels, by exactly the change of variables used in
\ref{fdip-005Z}. Integrating #{w'\circ O=w} gives the reduced-weight
identity; taking its real logarithm gives the interaction identity.
The full change of variables proves the normalizer and probability
statements. Orthogonal conjugacy preserves each determinant and
dimension. Applying the same reasoning to the transported induced
precision and weight proves the flag assertion.}
}

\p{Composition is covariant on fields and contravariant on
observables. If isometries form a left action #{L_gL_h=L_{gh}},
then their pushforwards obey #{(L_g)_*(L_h)_*=(L_{gh})_*}.
For #{T_gf=f\circ L_g}, evaluation gives
##{T_gT_hf=f\circ L_h\circ L_g=T_{hg}f.}
Consequently #{U_gf=f\circ L_{g^{-1}}} is the left representation
#{U_gU_h=U_{gh}}. No commutativity assumption is involved.
For preserved data, the law and fiber interaction are invariant;
transporting a selector alone does not transport #{Q} or #{V}.}

\p{This is the actual-integral counterpart of
\ref{fdip-005Z}, based on the conditional density in
\citet{Appendix A.2, (A.6), p. 200}{rasmussen2006gaussian}.
The supplied [background arrows](fdip-005C) give graph examples;
an arbitrary isometry need not preserve graph incidence.}
